            <ArticleTitle>[Angina pectoris as a symptom of gastric volvulus].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>236-9; 295</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Angina pectoris is a common complaint in any emergency service, but it rarely is of extracardiac origin. A rare case of gastric volvulus which presented a recurrent angina pectoris in a 47-year-old man is reported. Repeated episodes of angina pectoris in a patient with a negative cardiac investigation should alert the physician to the possibility of extracardiac pathology.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
